WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.519
OT telemetry is observable data produced by industrial systems, networks, devices, applications, and physical processes.

00:00:07.519 --> 00:00:17.317
It is the evidence that helps teams understand what exists, what is communicating, what changed, and how the operation responded.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:30.305
Telemetry can include network packets and flow records, device health events, controller status, process values, alarms, user authentication, application logs, remote-access sessions, file activity, and configuration changes.

00:00:30.305 --> 00:00:35.090
Each source describes a different layer of the environment.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:39.419
Raw telemetry is not the same as visibility.

00:00:39.419 --> 00:00:50.129
A record becomes useful when it has reliable time, asset identity, operational meaning, sufficient retention, and an owner who understands the source.

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:58.560
A device address or alarm code without context may be technically accurate but difficult to act upon.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:07.902
Security teams use telemetry to build inventory, establish expected behavior, design detections, investigate anomalies, reconstruct incidents, and verify architecture.

00:01:07.902 --> 00:01:16.788
Operations and engineering teams use many of the same records for troubleshooting, maintenance, quality, reliability, and performance analysis.

00:01:16.788 --> 00:01:19.750
Collection must respect OT constraints.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:25.902
Passive network monitoring and existing logs are often preferred near sensitive devices.

00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:36.612
New agents, active queries, and configuration changes should be tested and approved so visibility does not introduce latency, instability, or unsupported behavior.

00:01:36.612 --> 00:01:50.055
A telemetry program asks practical questions: what activity matters, which source can observe it, what is missing, how trustworthy is the data, and who responds when collection fails?

00:01:50.055 --> 00:01:59.853
Strong telemetry does not guarantee security, but it replaces assumptions with evidence that security, operations, and engineering can interpret together.
